ill open: The outgoing wallet recipient could be a pre-existing contact, payment broker, or unrelated wallet relationship rather than the person sought in the later email; another individual could be identified by authenticated contact records. P-1 supplies presented-device attribution, not independent physical ownership. — would change it: Authenticated intended-contact records or a specific cross-artifact link between the request and a named person would settle the required alternative; contradictory recipient/account evidence would change the persona attribution. — parts: alias "Disguise identity" established (E-54); requester "Person who asked Falcone to contact someone" established (E-23); intended "Intended contact and alternative formatted answer required by C-1" open, bounded by E-195 — premises: P-1 assumed (revision 1) — the premise tested against "the question's premise is not supported": The retained MIME contains an explicit contact instruction and sender/recipient; recipient account identity supports the presented mail persona. The independently authenticated wallet label does not identify the person meant by the separate request. (E-23, E-54, E-348, E-350) — reasoning: E-23 identifies Salvatore Maroni as requester; E-54 associates the recipient with Hugo Strange. Assuming P-1, this is the presented mail disguise. E-41 establishes the complete cached MIME attachment. E-150 corrects E-116's contextual memory/cache scope; E-107/E-109/E-112/E-113 and E-146 are dependent metadata, paths or reader echoes, not new actors or complete filesystem-area coverage. E-26 and E-143 document failed preliminary attempts only, while E-146 supplies the later successful corrected scan. E-20 bounds a preliminary parse, not successful MIME recovery. E-195 still bounds the unavailable DBX codec; E-289's readable-profile/sweep scope does not cover decrypted storage semantics. E-300 and E-295/E-298/E-299 concern unsuccessful partial decoding, not a global irrecoverability conclusion. E-303 establishes repaired-archive structure; E-212 establishes token construction rather than a key role. New E-324's seed literal scan is inconclusive for identity. E-326/E-327/E-328/E-330 recover source relationships, not contact names; E-329/E-331 independently check caller/getter source. E-332/E-334/E-337 establish seed serialization and validated decompression; E-336 supplies exact source constants. E-338/E-340 establish authenticated storage recovery, superseding the investigative gap but not contradicting E-320's differently keyed bounded test. E-348 independently establishes Oswald Cobblepot as the outgoing recipient-specific wallet label and Edward Nigma as the incoming top-level note; it is not a direct email/contact-database join. E-345/E-346's top-level-message checks do not refute the independently checked nested relation in E-348. E-350 shows the wallet's stored payment dates precede the request header, so a payment in response to this request cannot be inferred merely from a label. A pre-existing relationship remains possible. E-347's cached-profile private-key objects are not established as contact-client or DBX keys. These new findings materially advance provenance and yield a wallet candidate, but do not settle the C-1 intended-contact identity or justify an established formatted alternative. — by s42120100
